# Seals every stored launcher credential. Two forms:
#
#   hex:<64 hex chars>     32 bytes of real key material, used directly. Preferred.
#   <anything else>        treated as a passphrase and stretched with scrypt.
#
# Generate a raw key with:  echo "hex:$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
#
# The prefix is required. Raw keys are NOT detected by shape: a 43-character
# passphrase can decode to exactly 32 bytes, and guessing meant a low-entropy phrase
# was used verbatim as the key with the stretching skipped. If you do not use a
# prefix, you get the passphrase path — which is safe, just slower.
#
# Hex only, deliberately. base64 has three spellings of the same bytes (padded,
# unpadded, base64url), which needed a validator to tell truncation from a legitimate
# variant — and that validator rejected two of the three and stopped the app booting.
# Hex has one form, so there is nothing to get wrong.
#
# To CHANGE this value, do NOT just edit it — that would leave every stored
# credential unreadable. Run the rotation script first:
#
#   docker compose build backend      # the script ships only in images built
#                                     # from this change onwards
#   docker compose stop backend
#   docker compose run --rm \
#     -e GAMESHELF_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<current> \
#     -e GAMESHELF_ENCRYPTION_KEY_NEW=<new> \
#     backend node scripts/rotate-encryption-key.js
#
# then set this to the new value and start it again. Stop the app first: a sync in
# flight decrypts under the old key, spends seconds on a network refresh, and writes
# that credential back AFTER the rotation commits — under the old key, because that
# process still holds it. One launcher then becomes unreadable when you switch over.
GAMESHELF_ENCRYPTION_KEY=change_this_to_a_random_32_plus_char_string
GAMESHELF_JWT_SECRET=change_this_too
NODE_ENV=production
PORT=3001

# The passphrase salt is stored as "encryption-salt" in this directory, so back up
# the WHOLE directory, not just the .db file — the database alone cannot be
# decrypted without it. (No salt exists at all when the key is declared as
# hex:, and the boot log states which derivation is in force.)
#
# UPGRADING EXISTING CREDENTIALS IS OPTIONAL AND EXPLICIT. Starting this release
# changes nothing on disk: credentials written by earlier versions keep working,
# because reads dispatch on the stored format. Rolling the image back is therefore
# safe. To move them onto the salted derivation, run the rotation script ONCE with the
# new key set to your CURRENT key:
#
#   docker compose stop backend
#   docker compose run --rm \
#     -e GAMESHELF_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<current> \
#     -e GAMESHELF_ENCRYPTION_KEY_NEW=<current> \
#     backend node scripts/rotate-encryption-key.js
#
# It verifies every credential re-opens before reporting success. From that point the
# salt file is required, so back up this whole directory rather than gameshelf.db
# alone — and a rollback to an older image would need the backup.
GAMESHELF_DB_PATH=/app/data/gameshelf.db
IGDB_CLIENT_ID=
IGDB_CLIENT_SECRET=
STEAMGRIDDB_API_KEY=

# Lancache orchestrator integration (F14). Leave unset to disable the cache UI.
ORCH_API_URL=http://192.168.1.40:8765
ORCH_TOKEN=

# Docker frontend configuration
# For development: FRONTEND_TARGET=dev FRONTEND_PORT=5173 FRONTEND_INTERNAL_PORT=5173
# For production (default): FRONTEND_TARGET=prod FRONTEND_PORT=80 FRONTEND_INTERNAL_PORT=80
FRONTEND_TARGET=prod
FRONTEND_PORT=80
FRONTEND_INTERNAL_PORT=80
